      "title": "Iran’s Provincial Planning Councils | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the documented responsibilities of provincial Planning and Development Councils, their secretariats, and specialist working groups."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "legal-role",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A forum for provincial development decisions",
        "content": "The institution usually shortened here to a provincial planning council is the *Shora-ye Barnamerizi va Tose'e-ye Ostan*, or Provincial Planning and Development Council. Article 31 of the Permanent Provisions of Development Programs Law describes it as the highest provincial decision-making and supervisory body for economic, social, and cultural development. Its responsibilities nevertheless operate within national planning and policy constraints. [s1]\n\nThe council considers provincial development documents, monitors balanced development, and addresses investment coordination. Its membership brings the governor and planning organization together with provincial officials; parliamentary representatives participate as observers. This is an administrative development council, and its statutory responsibilities should not be confused with those of an elected municipal council. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "budget-boundaries",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The boundary between province and county",
        "content": "Article 31 limits the council’s role in the specified county allocations: it determines county totals for capital-asset appropriations and the referenced balanced-development funding using population and deprivation indicators. The provision expressly bars interference in county planning committee decisions. This distinction matters when identifying which body is responsible for a particular allocation. [s1]\n\nThe implementing regulation also assigns the council consideration of provincial operating-budget distribution and policies for selecting provincial capital projects. These provisions concern defined public-budget responsibilities; they do not establish control over every investment made within a province. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "working-method",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How proposals reach the council",
        "content": "The inspected regulation organizes specialist work around economic affairs and investment; infrastructure, settlements, spatial planning and environment; social affairs and health; and education, research and innovation. Working-group decisions are proposals requiring council approval under the relevant rules. The secretariat follows implementation and prepares periodic performance reports. [s2]\n\nThis distinction provides a useful way to read meeting reports: a working-group recommendation and a council approval represent different stages. Neither, by itself, proves that expenditure occurred or a project was completed.",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
